-> A scalar's finite-but-unresolved domain already denotes a set of
-> possible values. `∪`/`∩` see it that way instead of demanding the
-> scalar be pinned first — an unresolved-but-finite domain and an
-> explicit Set literal both count as the same kind of thing here.
a = {5}
b ∈ Z
b > 3
b < 6
-> b's domain is {4, 5}; neither `b` itself nor `a` is touched by this.
c = a ∪ b
assert c = {4, 5}

-> Both sides can be unresolved-but-finite at once.
p ∈ Z
p > 0
p < 3
q ∈ Z
q > 3
q < 6
assert (p ∪ q) = {1, 2, 4, 5}

-> ∩ narrows to the actual overlap between two ranges.
x ∈ Z
x > 0
x < 6
y ∈ Z
y > 3
y < 8
assert (x ∩ y) = {4, 5}

-> A `ValueSet` domain (from `∈ {..}`, not just a range) works the same way.
v ∈ { 1, 2, 3 }
assert (v ∪ {4}) = {1, 2, 3, 4}

-> Materializing a domain never resolves the variable itself — it's still
-> usable unpinned afterward, same as `loop <var> { }` (T26 Phase 1).
n ∈ Z
n > 10
n < 13
also = n ∪ {99}
assert also = {11, 12, 99}
n = 11
assert n = 11
